    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1067">

                <pb facs="1242" n="1046" xml:id="pg1046"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">868.00/7–2445: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 1067<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kennan</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d1067-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-07-24T16:00:00+03:00">July 24, 1945—4 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2667. Vice Commissar FonOff
                    Dekanozov addressed letter dated July 13
                    to British Chargé<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1067-f2"/> in reply to British
                        Ambassador’s<anchor n="3" xml:id="d1067-f3"/> protest to Molotov (Embassy’s 2472, July 7<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d1067-f4"/>) re Soviet press and radio campaign against Greek Govt
                    and British in Greece (To Dept 2667 repeated
                    London 378 Athens 37).</p>
                <p>Dekanozov stated Soviet Govt does not see
                    any grounds for Clark Kerr charges re
                        <hi rendition="#italic">New Times</hi> article as journal not an official
                    organ of Soviet Govt. Furthermore Soviet Govt finds it necessary to call British
                    Govt’s attention to Athens July 8 Fascist meeting where sharply hostile speeches
                    to USSR were made and slogans of “let[’]s get at Sofia, Belgrade, Moscow” etc[.]
                    were uttered. Such speeches becoming more frequent and in increasingly
                    inadmissible forms Dekanozov claimed.</p>
                <p>He pointed out Soviet Govt regarded such situation wholly incorrect in country
                    where there are troops of USSR’s British Ally.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Kennan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1067-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d1067-f1-content">
                <p>The gist of this message was included in telegram No. 126 of July 25 from
                        Grew to Byrnes (file No. 800.00 Summaries/7–2545).</p>
            </note>
